 ## Background
-The concept of removing interior polygons to improve rendering performance was introduced in the context of voxel-based procedural terrain generation, specifically referencing Minecraft-like worlds [S1, unofficial]. The initial optimization involved reconstructing cubes from triangles and checking for solid neighbors to avoid rendering hidden sides [S1, unofficial]. This led to the combination of cube meshes into single "chunks" to optimize implementation, although this introduced engine-specific vertex limits (e.g., Unity's ~65,000 vertex limit per mesh) [S1, unofficial]. The technique has since evolved into various specialized forms, including bitmask optimizations for buried blocks [S2, unofficial], two-pass transparent meshing [S3, unofficial], and opacity-based culling for neural rendering [S4]. From-region visibility preprocessing, while applicable to 3D environments, has been noted for its high computational cost [S5, unofficial].
+The concept of removing interior polygons to improve rendering performance originated in early voxel-based procedural terrain generation systems, such as those used in Minecraft-like engines [S1, unofficial]. The initial brute-force approach of instantiating full cube models resulted in unnecessary rendering of hidden triangles; for example, rendering 12 triangles (6 sides) when only 2 triangles (1 side) were visible [S1, unofficial]. To address this, a class was added to reconstruct cubes from triangles and check for solid neighbors, preventing the rendering of sides adjacent to those neighbors [S1, unofficial]. This optimization was further enhanced by combining the meshes of all individual cubes into a single big mesh called a 'chunk' [S1, unofficial]. However, this introduced engine-specific restrictions, such as Unity's limit of ~65,000 vertices per mesh, requiring chunks to be stacked or split to achieve larger volumes [S1, unofficial].
 
